WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.192
We are six students from Bangladesh. One
team, one obsession.

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.688
Six students, six roles,

3
00:00:09.728 --> 00:00:15.376
turning an invisible medical risk into
something an astronaut can actually see.

4
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:26.055
We did not want to fake a
spaceship dashboard.

5
00:00:26.095 --> 00:00:30.160
We wanted to answer one honest question:
am I still me today?

6
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.736
Six months in microgravity. Then
gravity comes back.

7
00:00:39.026 --> 00:00:44.026
After months of weightlessness, the inner
ear has miscalibrated.

8
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.968
The ground tilts while the astronaut
is perfectly still.

9
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:58.944
Dizziness, disorientation, a delayed hand.
The risk peaks the moment gravity returns.

10
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.376
And help is not close: up to twenty two
minutes one way.

11
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.880
So the crew must read their own
bodies. Honestly. Alone.

12
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.504
So we built the instrument for
that moment.

13
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.420
We do not compare the astronaut to
an average human.

14
00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:29.816
We compare them to yesterday's self.

15
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:39.096
Six domains, from balance and reaction to
eye head coordination.

16
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:45.732
They fold into one score, with its
uncertainty beside it,

17
00:01:45.772 --> 00:01:48.160
and a noise floor called MDC.

18
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:55.568
It runs in the browser, fully offline.
No ground link.

19
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:04.792
This is not a mockup. Calibrate, capture a
baseline, run a test.

20
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.468
Twenty five bursts, ninety two
percent correct,

21
00:02:11.508 --> 00:02:14.752
median reaction five forty
two milliseconds.

22
00:02:15.042 --> 00:02:20.066
It never says you are unfit. It says: this
task is risky now.

23
00:02:20.106 --> 00:02:23.138
Run the protocol, then measure again.

24
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.560
Real instruments connect through an
open contract.

25
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.142
And when the camera cannot see the eyes,
we do not invent a zero.

26
00:02:35.182 --> 00:02:36.896
We write: unavailable.

27
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:42.215
The engine is finished. What comes
next needs hardware:

28
00:02:42.255 --> 00:02:46.624
eye tracking, force plates, a
crew dashboard, validation.

29
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.864
Keyed to NASA's Human Research Program.

30
00:02:50.154 --> 00:02:53.738
We measure what we can, and we are honest
about the rest.

